y already covered by the bibliography\"\n      ],\n      \"queries\": [\n        {\n          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n          \"query\": \"Chatbot Arena open platform for evaluating LLMs by human preference\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Target arena-leaderboard-context (Introduction, Data): the opening claim that Bradley-Terry leaderboards are used to choose and announce models, and the Arena 55k/140k data, have no platform citation.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n          \"query\": \"Judging LLM-as-a-judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena human pairwise judgments\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Target arena-leaderboard-context (Data): the MT-Bench human expert judgments and the Arena human-preference data release are used without citing the dataset\n\n[truncated after 14000 chars]\n```\n\n## manuscript source excerpt\n\n```tex\n\\documentclass{article}\n\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}\n}\n\n \n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\newcommand{\\kcap}{k_{\\mathrm{cap}}}\n\\newcommand{\\hi}{h}\n\\newcommand{\\lo}{\\ell}\n\n\\title{How Few Comparisons Flip a Bradley--Terry Leaderboard Pair? Refit-Verified Removal Budgets on Crowd-Vote and Sports Data}\n\\author{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPublic leaderboards built from pairwise comparisons fit a Bradley--Terry model and publish a ranking, yet how many comparisons must change before a ranking reverses is rarely measured. We study the smallest set of deleted comparisons, within a cap of 2\\% of all rows, that reverses the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair after an exact ridge-regularised Bradley--Terry refit. We measure it on a crowd-vote table (Chatbot Arena, 135,630 comparisons) and an NBA game table (12,900 comparisons). Verified budgets are small: 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of the crowd table (at most 0.046\\% of comparisons) and 62 and 8 rows for the sports table (0.48\\% and 0.062\\%). Mid-table pairs are cheaper than top pairs, and random deletion flips the top pairs in 0 of 20 draws. However, a static first-order influence ranking with refit bisection reaches every one of these budgets, so the more elaborate search we studied adds no measurable gain, and the crowd-versus-sports contrast is confounded with table size, participants and era. A six-model expert-judgment table did not flip within the cap in development runs, for an unknown reason. All findings are descriptive: four cases, two designs, one rating model.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nLeaderboards that aggregate ``which of A and B is better'' judgments into Bradley--Terry (BT) ratings are used to choose models, to announce releases, and to compare research systems; Chatbot Arena is a prominent crowd-vote example \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132}. The BT model and its many extensions are decades old \\citep{Davidson1970OnET,Davidson1969MultivariatePC,Leonard1977AnAB}, and the statistical properties of its estimates under sampling noise are increasingly well understood \\citep{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT,Gao2021UncertaintyQI,Li2021ThePO}. What is less often reported is a complementary, \\emph{combinatorial} question: how much of the underlying comparison table has to change, whether through noise or deliberate action, before a published rank order reverses?\n\nWe make this question operational. Given a comparison table and one adjacent pair of rated participants, the \\emph{removal budget} is the smallest number of rows whose deletion makes an exact refit of the same BT objective rank the lower participant above the upper one. Deletion is a deliberately weak perturbation: it neither inserts nor relabels votes, and it is closely related to the ``dropping a little data'' robustness question of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}. Because every proposed deletion set is checked by an exact refit, any reported budget is a certificate that a reversal is attainable with that many rows, and therefore an upper bound on the true minimum.\n\n\\paragraph{Research question.} Under an exact BT refit (ridge $0.01$, ties as half wins), what is the smallest removal set within a cap of $2\\%$ of all rows that reverses an adjacent top-of-table pair or an adjacent mid-table pair, and does this budget differ between a crowd-vote table and a sports-results table when both are measured by one verified protocol?\n\n\\paragraph{What we do.} We measure verified removal budgets on two held-out tables of real data: a deduplicated Chatbot Arena crowd-vote table and an NBA game-results table. We compare the studied search configuration, which orders rows by first-order influence on the rating gap and then refines the set with re-linearised greedy batches and a local polish, against three evaluator-owned comparators (random removal, first-order influence only, and opponent-targeted removal) and three validity controls (empty set, label permutation, cross-table copy). A separate pair of development tables, including a six-model expert-judgment table, is used to compare search configurations and is reported separately.\n\n\\paragraph{What we find.} (i) Budgets are small in all four held-out cases (4 to 62 of 135,630 crowd rows; 8 to 62 of 12,900 NBA rows). (ii) Mid pairs are cheaper than top pairs in both tables. (iii) Measured as a share of comparisons, the sports budgets are about 10 and 21 times larger (top and mid), but the two top-pair budgets are the same number of rows (62), so this contrast largely reflects table size and is confounded with participants and era. (iv) A one-shot influence ranking with refit bisection already attains every budget; the additional search machinery produced no measured improvement on held-out cases and none on the development tables. (v) On the six-model expert-judgment development table, no flip was found within the cap, and our evidence cannot say whether that is an inherent property of the data or a limitation of the search.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.} (1) A precisely specified, evaluator-verified removal-budget protocol with comparators and controls, whose budgets are certificates rather than estimates. (2) Measured budgets for four held-out cases on two evaluation designs, with the crowd-versus-sports contrast and its confounds spelled out. (3) A negative result: first-order influence ordering with exact-refit bisection matches the more elaborate configuration everywhere we measured. (4) An explicit scope statement: the claims concern the declared cases only, not confidence-interval-based rank changes, adversarial insertion or vote rigging, other rating models, or causal effects of evaluation design.\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Bradley--Terry models and their estimation.} The BT model has been extended to ties and within-pair order effects \\citep{Davidson1970OnET,Davidson1977OnET}, to multivariate and two-dimensional comparisons \\citep{Davidson1969MultivariatePC,Causeur2005A2E}, to continuous outcomes and generalised score estimation \\citep{Stern2011ModeratedPC,Fageot2023GeneralizedBM}, to covariates and sparse grouped designs \\citep{Yan2025InferenceIA,Yan2011GROUPEDSP}, and to Bayesian formulations \\citep{Leonard1977AnAB,Hussain2013BayesianIO,Rizvi2026BayesianBF}. Conditions for existence of the maximum likelihood estimate and its consistency are studied by \\citet{Butler2004TheEO} and \\citet{Bong2021GeneralizedRF}, and ridge-penalised fitting is analysed by \\citet{Varin2024TractableRR}. Computational work covers gradient and minorise--maximise iterations \\citep{Vojnović2019ConvergenceRO,Sawadogo2019CombiningMA}, and relations to priority derivation and to PageRank are drawn by \\citet{Genest1999DerivingPF} and \\citet{Selby2024PageRankAT}. We use none of these extensions: our evaluator fixes a plain ridge-penalised BT objective in which a tie counts as half a win, a convention that differs from explicit tie models such as that of \\citet{Davidson1970OnET}. The ridge term keeps every refit well defined after rows are removed.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty and rank inference.} Sampling-based uncertainty for BT scores and ranks is the subject of \\citet{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}, \\citet{Makur2024MinimaxHT} (hypothesis testing), \\citet{Morel-Balbi2025EstimationOP} (partial rankings from sparse, noisy comparisons) and \\citet{Li2021ThePO} (estimation error under general comparison graphs). These works ask how sampling variability limits what a ranking can resolve. We instead fix the observed table and ask how few of its rows determine the order of one adjacent pair. This is a statement about a single realised data set, not about a population, and we report no confidence-interval-based rank events.\n\n\\paragraph{Data-dropping robustness.} \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF} propose an automatic finite-sample metric for when removing a small fraction of data changes a statistical conclusion, and show it on published applied analyses. Our setting is a specific instance in spirit: the conclusion is the order of one adjacent pair under a BT fit, and the dropped fraction is capped at $2\\%$. Unlike an approximation-only metric, we accept a removal set only after an exact refit reverses the pair. \\citet{Defresne2025PreferenceEF} use maximum-likelihood preference models for active elicitation, a different goal from auditing a fixed table.\n\n\\paragraph{Manipulation of crowd-vote leaderboards.} \\citet{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2501.17858} study vote-rigging strategies against Chatbot Arena and their effect on model rankings. Their setting is adversarial and allows an actor to add votes, whereas ours is a deletion-only perturbation checked by exact refit, with no adversary model, so the two are complementary rather than comparable and we do not compare numbers. Influence-based analysis of single training points is the tool behind our row ordering \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730}. Work on other evaluation practices of arena-style platforms, and on MT-Bench-style model-judge evaluation, is relevant background that is not in our verified reference set, and we make no claim of novelty relative to it.\n\n\\section{Problem Setup and Method}\n\\subsection{Bradley--Terry leaderboards and the removal budget}\nA comparison table has $N$ rows $(a_i,b_i,y_i)$ over $M$ participants, where $y_i\\in\\{\\text{$a$ wins},\\text{$b$ wins},\\text{tie}\\}$ is encoded as a score $s_i\\in\\{1,0,\\tfrac12\\}$. For ratings $\\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^M$ and $d_i=\\theta_{a_i}-\\theta_{b_i}$ the fit minimises\n\\begin{equation}\nL(\\theta)=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\Big[\\log\\!\\big(1+e^{d_i}\\big)-s_i d_i\\Big]+\\tfrac{\\lambda}{2}\\lVert\\theta\\rVert^2,\\qquad \\lambda=0.01,\n\\label{eq:bt}\n\\end{equation}\nand participants are ranked by $\\theta$. Let $\\hi$ be ranked directly above $\\lo$. For a removal set $R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,N\\}$ let $\\theta^{(-R)}$ be the exact minimiser of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt} on the remaining rows. The pair \\emph{flips} if $\\theta^{(-R)}_{\\lo}>\\theta^{(-R)}_{\\hi}$. With cap $\\kcap=\\lceil 0.02N\\rceil$, the \\emph{removal budget} is\n\\begin{equation}\nk^\\star=\\min\\{|R|:\\ |R|\\le\\kcap,\\ \\text{pair flips under }R\\},\n\\end{equation}\nand a search procedure is scored by $1-k/\\kcap$ if it returns a flipping set of size $k\\le\\kcap$ and $0$ otherwise. Because the score is linear in $k$, a score near $1$ means a budget far below the cap. We report budgets in two units: rows, and percent of all $N$ comparisons (derived as $k/N$). The evaluator's ``budget fraction'' metric is $k/\\kcap$, a fraction of the allowance and not of the table.\n\nTwo kinds of target are used. The \\emph{top} pair is ranks 1 and 2 of the full-table fit; the \\emph{mid} pair is ranks $\\lfloor M/2\\rfloor$ and $\\lfloor M/2\\rfloor+1$. A flip here is a reversal of the \\emph{point-estimate} order; no confidence-interval rank event is considered.\n\n\\subsection{Why influence ordering should help, and what we test}\nDeleting row $i$ perturbs the stationarity condition by $-\\nabla\\ell_i$, so a Newton step predicts the change of the gap $g=\\theta_{\\hi}-\\theta_{\\lo}$ as, in the manner of influence-function analyses of the effect of removing a training point \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730},\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta_i\\;\\approx\\;\\frac{(p_i-s_i)\\,(v_{a_i}-v_{b_i})}{1-w_i\\,q_i},\\qquad v=H^{-1}(e_{\\hi}-e_{\\lo}),\n\\label{eq:infl}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $p_i=\\sigma(d_i)$, $w_i=p_i(1-p_i)$, $H$ is the Hessian of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt} (including the ridge), and $q_i=x_i^\\top H^{-1}x_i$ with $x_i=e_{a_i}-e_{b_i}$ gives the leave-one-out leverage correction. Rows with the most negative $\\Delta_i$ close the gap fastest to first order. Our use of this approximation differs from a pure influence analysis in that it only orders candidate rows, and every set is then verified by an exact refit. The expectation is that removing the most negative rows first needs far fewer deletions than undirected removal. Eq.~\\eqref{eq:infl} is a linearisation, so the question we test is twofold: whether an influence order is enough to reach the exact-refit budget, and whether re-linearising and polishing it reduces the budget further.\n\n\\subsection{Studied configuration}\nThe studied configuration (source in the staged evidence for the selected candidate) fits BT by damped Newton iterations with backtracking and an analytic gradient and dense Hessian, and then proceeds in three steps. (a) It ranks rows by Eq.~\\eqref{eq:infl} at the full-table fit and finds, by bisection over prefixes of that order with an exact refit at each probe, the smallest prefix of at most $\\kcap$ rows that flips the pair. (b) It runs a re-linearised greedy search: after each batch of removals it refits, recomputes Eq.~\\eqref{eq:infl} on the remaining rows, and removes a batch of $\\lceil 0.6\\,n_{\\mathrm{lin}}\\rceil$ rows, where $n_{\\mathrm{lin}}$ is the number of rows the linearised gains need to close the current gap; once the pair flips it bisects over prefixes of the insertion order. (c) It polishes the best set by drop-and-readd local search: it drops the least useful quarter of the incumbent set (halving that fraction after a failure, for at most 14 rounds), refills greedily under a strictly smaller size limit, and keeps any strictly smaller verified set. A set is accepted only if its own exact refit separates $\\theta_{\\lo}$ above $\\theta_{\\hi}$ by a margin of $10^{-5}$; the evaluator then re-verifies independently. Search stops at 60\\% of the allotted deadline. Bisection over prefixes assumes that longer prefixes flip once shorter ones do; this is not guaranteed, so the returned $k$ is a verified upper bound on the minimum and minimality is not claimed.\n\n\\subsection{Comparators and validity controls}\nThree comparators are computed by the evaluator on the same tables and scored with the same rule. \\textbf{B1, random removal:} 20 random row orders, each truncated to $\\kcap$ rows and bisected over prefixes; we report the number of draws that flip. \\textbf{B2, influence only:} one static ranking by the uncorrected first-order influence at the full-table fit, followed by exact-refit prefix bisection; this is step (a) without leverage correction, re-linearisation or polishing. \\textbf{B3, opponent-targeted removal:} delete rows in which the upper-ranked participant beat another participant, ordered by the opponent's rating from strongest downwards, then bisect over prefixes; this is a plausible hand-built attack that needs no gradient.\n\nThree controls guard against vacuous results. The \\emph{empty set} must not flip any case. A \\emph{label-permutation} control shuffles outcomes across rows of the same table (fixed seed) and targets the top pair of the permuted fit; it is accepted as non-vacuous only if the fraction of moved labels is within 90\\% of its expectation and the rating spread falls to at most half of the real one. The \\emph{cross-table copy} control applies the removal set found for one table's top pair to the other table's top pair after rescaling row indices; a flip would signal that results depend on row position rather than data content.\n\n\\subsection{Why these definitions}\nThe perturbation scale and the cross-dataset comparison are choices, so we state the reasons for them. \\emph{Deletion only:} every remaining row is a genuine observation, so a flip cannot be blamed on fabricated data, and an exact refit turns each deletion set into a checkable certificate. Insertion, relabelling and vote rigging would require an adversary model that we do not specify. \\emph{Adjacent pairs:} if a removal set puts $\\lo$ above $\\hi$ and the original ranking placed other participants between them, then at least one adjacent pair in that chain must also have reversed. The smallest budget over adjacent pairs therefore lower-bounds the budget of any non-adjacent reversal, and adjacent pairs are the cheapest rank events. \\emph{Top and mid:} the top pair is the position that leaderboards publicise, and the mid pair probes a typical interior position. \\emph{A 2\\% cap:} it fixes a common tolerance for how much of a table noise or an actor could plausibly alter and makes scores comparable across tables of different size. It is a convention of the evaluator, not an estimate of any real process, and we did not vary it; all held-out budgets sit well below it except the sports top pair, which uses 24\\% of its allowance. \n\\emph{Share of the table:} budgets are reported as a share of all rows because that is the unit in which a fixed-fraction perturbation is defined; Section~\\ref{sec:contrast} also gives a per-participant view.\n\n\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n\\subsection{Data}\nTable~\\ref{tab:data} lists the tables. All rows are anonymised before reaching the search procedure: participants become integers, rows are provided as unsigned 16-bit participant indices and an 8-bit outcome, in at most four chunks. The held-out crowd table is the Chatbot Arena 140k preference release (data from the platform of \\citealp{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132}) after content-hash deduplication against the development tables: from 135,634 raw rows, 2 were removed for content overlap with development data (a further 652 rows are listed in the evaluator metadata as prompt-only overlaps and were kept) and 2 rows of participants with fewer than 100 rows were dropped, leaving 135,630 rows over 52 models. The sports table holds NBA game results from 2006 to 2015 with 12,900 comparisons over 30 teams. The development tables are the Chatbot Arena 55k release (57,477 rows, 64 models) and the MT-Bench human-judgment table (3,355 rows, 6 models), the latter standing in for expert annotation. Dataset commits and licences come from the public task brief and are listed in the Supplement. Model-judge data were not used.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Comparison tables. Caps are $\\lceil 0.02N\\rceil$ (derived). Held-out tables were never used to choose a search configuration; development tables were.}\n\\label{tab:data}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llllrrr}\n\\toprule\nUse & Design & Table & Rows $N$ & Participants & Cap $\\kcap$\\\\\n\\midrule\nHeld-out & crowd votes & Arena 140k (deduplicated) & 135,630 & 52 models & 2,713\\\\\nHeld-out & sports & NBA 2006--2015 & 12,900 & 30 teams & 258\\\\\nDevelopment & crowd votes & Arena 55k & 57,477 & 64 models & 1,150\\\\\nDevelopment & expert judgments & MT-Bench human & 3,355 & 6 models & 68\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\n\n\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cases, seeds and reported uncertainty}\nEach table contributes two cases, a top pair and a mid pair, giving four held-out cases (crowd: F1 top, F2 mid; sports: F3 top, F4 mid) plus two permutation controls (F5, F6) on the top pairs. The held-out evaluation ran two seeded episodes (seeds 21 and 22); the development evaluation ran two (seeds 11 and 12). A family score is the mean of the case scores within a design, and the overall score is the mean of the two family scores. Uncertainty is limited by design: there are two episodes, B1 has 20 random draws per case, and the two held-out episodes returned identical per-case budgets. We therefore report no confidence intervals or significance tests, and nothing here is a population-level interval over data sets. Wall-clock figures are evaluator-recorded durations.\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation scopes and qualification}\nSearch configurations were scored on the development tables, and one frozen configuration was then evaluated in a single sealed held-out evaluation. We keep the two scopes in separate tables and sections and never combine their scores. No search configuration exceeded the starting baseline on the development tables (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}), so the frozen selection is not a winner; it is one of three configurations with identical development scores. Its held-out evaluation completed and was recorded as qualified. We read this as: the evaluation ran to completion and its controls passed. It is not evidence of superiority over the starting baseline, which was not run on the held-out tables, and it does not extend the findings beyond the declared cases.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}","numLines":738,"startLine":1,"totalLines":895,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}}}
